#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>
#include <mutex>

void insertIntoVector(std::unordered_map<int, std::vector<int>> &map, int key, int value) {
    // Use a mutex to ensure thread safety when modifying the map
    static std::mutex mtx;
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);

    // Check if the key exists in the map
    auto it = map.find(key);
    if (it != map.end()) {
        // If the key exists, insert the value into the corresponding vector
        it->second.push_back(value);
    } else {
        // If the key does not exist, create a new vector and insert the value
        std::vector<int> vec;
        vec.push_back(value);
        map[key] = vec;
    }
}
```
This implementation uses a mutex to ensure thread safety when modifying the `std::unordered_map`. The mutex is used to lock the map for the duration of the insertion operation, preventing concurrent modifications to the same key.

The function first checks if the key exists in the map using the `find` method. If it does exist, the value is inserted into the corresponding vector using the `push_back` method. If the key does not exist, a new vector is created and the value is inserted into this new vector using the `push_back` method. Finally, the new vector is added to the map using the `[key] = vec` syntax.

This implementation ensures efficient insertion and retrieval operations by minimizing the number of unnecessary operations and avoiding unnecessary overhead. It also handles potential concurrency issues by using a mutex to ensure thread safety when modifying the map. Additionally, it optimizes memory usage by creating new vectors only when necessary, rather than creating a new vector for every key-value pair.

Finally, this implementation is maintainable and scalable as the dataset grows by using a simple and easy-to-understand data structure, such as a `std::unordered_map`.']

