['  Here's an example of a thread-safe C++ function that randomly selects one of two attacks for a video game character to use, with a cooldown period between attacks. The function takes no arguments and returns nothing. The cooldown period is 9 seconds, and the function prints the selected attack number to the console. Additionally, the function manages multiple game characters concurrently without overlapping cooldowns.
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <mutex>
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>

using namespace std;

// Global variable to store the cooldown timers for each character
map<int, chrono::steady_clock::time_point> characterCooldownTimers;

void attack(int characterId) {
    // Check if any other thread is currently in the attack function
    static mutex attackMutex;
    unique_lock<mutex> lock(attackMutex);

    // Get the current time point
    auto now = chrono::steady_clock::now();

    // Check if any other thread is currently in the cooldown timer for this character
    if (characterCooldownTimers.find(characterId) != characterCooldownTimers.end()) {
        // If another thread is currently in the cooldown timer, check if it has expired
        if (now >= characterCooldownTimers[characterId]) {
            // If the cooldown timer has expired, clear it and proceed with the attack
            characterCooldownTimers.erase(characterId);
        } else {
            // If the cooldown timer has not expired, block until it does
            while (now < characterCooldownTimers[characterId]) {
                this_thread::sleep_for(chrono::seconds(1));
                now = chrono::steady_clock::now();
            }
        }
    }

    // Select an attack at random
    int attackNumber = rand() % 2 + 1;

    // Print the selected attack to the console
    cout << "Character " << characterId << " attacks with " << attackNumber << endl;

    // Set the cooldown timer for this character
    characterCooldownTimers[characterId] = chrono::steady_clock::now() + chrono::seconds(9);
}
```
This implementation uses a map data structure to store the cooldown timers for each character. The attack function uses a mutex to ensure that only one thread can enter the function at a time, regardless of how many characters are currently using it. When a thread enters the function, it checks if any other thread is currently in the cooldown timer for this character. If another thread is already in the cooldown timer, the current thread blocks until the cooldown timer expires. Once the attack selection process is complete, the function sets the cooldown timer for this character and exits.

This implementation uses a simple mutex to protect access to the global data structure that stores the cooldown timers. However, it may not be necessary to use a mutex in all cases, as the attack function only reads and writes the cooldown timers without modifying them. In this case, using an atomic variable or a std::atomic<chrono::steady_clock::time_point> instead of a mutex could provide better performance.

Additionally, to improve performance, it may be beneficial to use a thread-safe container such as std::vector or std::set to store the cooldown timers for each character. This would allow multiple threads to concurrently access and modify the cooldown timers without blocking other threads.

In summary, this implementation is thread-safe and fair, and it ensures that no two characters can attack simultaneously within their cooldown periods. However, using an atomic variable or a std::atomic<chrono::steady_clock::time_point> may provide better performance in certain cases, and using a thread-safe container to store the cooldown timers for each character could further improve performance.']

