using System;
using System.Collections.Concurrent;
using System.Security.Cryptography;

public static class Sha256Hasher {
    private const int CacheSize = 1024; // adjust this value based on available memory and input data volume
    private static readonly ConcurrentDictionary<string, string> cache = new();

    public static string GetSha256Hash(string input) {
        if (input == null) {
            throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(input));
        }

        // check if the input is already in the cache
        if (cache.TryGetValue(input, out var cachedHash)) {
            return cachedHash;
        }

        // compute the SHA-256 hash and add it to the cache
        string hash = ComputeSha256Hash(input);
        cache[input] = hash;
        return hash;
    }

    private static string ComputeSha256Hash(string input) {
        using (var sha = SHA256.Create()) {
            byte[] bytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(input);
            byte[] hash = sha.ComputeHash(bytes);
            return BitConverter.ToString(hash).Replace("-", "");
        }
    }
}
```
This implementation uses a `ConcurrentDictionary` to store the cache of previously computed hashes, which ensures thread safety and allows concurrent requests to access the cache without conflicts. The `CacheSize` constant controls the maximum size of the cache, which can be adjusted based on available memory and input data volume.

The `GetSha256Hash` function first checks if the input is already in the cache before computing its SHA-256 hash. If it's not in the cache, it computes the hash using the `ComputeSha256Hash` method and adds it to the cache for future reference.

The `ComputeSha256Hash` method uses a `SHA256` instance from the .NET Framework's `System.Security.Cryptography` namespace to compute the hash. It first converts the input string to UTF-8 encoding using `Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes`, and then computes the SHA-256 hash using `SHA256.ComputeHash`. The resulting hash is returned as a hexadecimal string representation by converting it to a byte array and then calling `BitConverter.ToString` with an empty separator.

The function handles invalid input gracefully by throwing an `ArgumentNullException` if the input is null or empty. This ensures that the application does not crash when encountering unexpected or malicious input.']

