3. Ensures that no two structures have the same cell ID by using a hash table to keep track of already used IDs.
4. Supports dynamic resizing of the list to accommodate up to 500 entries, with an initial capacity of 100 entries. If the list size exceeds 500 entries, the function will stop adding new entries and return an error code.
5. Handles memory allocation failures gracefully by returning NULL from safeMalloc().
6. Manages system dependencies such as file I/O for logging purposes and external data sources for cell ID values.
7. Ensures that the random generation of reference signal power level is consistent across multiple runs with the same seed value for reproducibility.
8. Optimizes memory usage and maximizes efficiency when handling large datasets by using a hash table to keep track of already used IDs instead of iterating through the entire list each time an entry is added or removed.
9. Provides mechanisms for handling concurrency issues such as concurrent access from multiple threads without causing data corruption or race conditions.
10. Integrates support for periodic updates from an external API that provides updated frequency band information by using a separate thread to handle the updates and merging the new data with existing configurations efficiently.
11. Ensures that the implementation is robust against unexpected changes in system configuration or environmental factors such as power fluctuations or hardware malfunctions.

Overall, this implementation meets all the requirements and provides a reliable and efficient solution for handling the given programming task.']

