To track the performance of the function under different load conditions, this implementation uses a simple performance counter that is incremented for each PDU allocated in the list. The performance counter can be used to monitor the rate at which PDUs are being allocated and deallocated, and to detect any performance bottlenecks or issues.

To manage potential race conditions if multiple threads attempt to populate the list simultaneously, this implementation uses a mutex lock to synchronize access to the list of PDUs. The mutex lock ensures that only one thread can modify the list at a time, which prevents race conditions from occurring.

Overall, this implementation should be efficient in terms of both time and space complexity, as it avoids unnecessary memory allocation and uses a dynamic array to handle varying sizes of input data efficiently. The use of a mutex lock and a simple performance counter also helps to ensure that the function can handle high levels of concurrency without excessive overhead or performance issues.']

