Here is a possible implementation of the `ContainsSubstringInHash` function that meets the requirements:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;

public class HashChecker
{
    public bool ContainsSubstringInHash(string input, string substring)
    {
        if (input == null || input.Length == 0)
        {
            return false;
        }

        using (var md5 = MD5.Create())
        {
            var hash = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(md5.ComputeHash(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(input)));

            if (substring == null || substring.Length == 0)
            {
                return false;
            }

            var substringBytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(substring);
            var substringHash = md5.ComputeHash(substringBytes);

            for (int i = 0; i < hash.Length; i++)
            {
                if (hash[i] == substringHash[i])
                {
                    return true;
                }
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```
This implementation uses the `MD5` class from the .NET Framework to generate the MD5 hash of the input string. It then checks if the substring is present in the hash using a simple loop. The function also handles null or empty strings gracefully by returning `false`.

To optimize the function for high traffic, it's important to minimize the amount of overhead and unnecessary computation. One way to achieve this is to use the `using` statement to create a new instance of the `MD5` class and dispose of it when it's no longer needed. This helps to avoid memory leaks and other performance issues.

Another optimization would be to use a constant-time algorithm for comparing the hashes, such as the `constantTimeEquals` method from the `Security.Cryptography.HashAlgorithm` class. This ensures that the comparison is performed in a way that's resistant to timing attacks.
```csharp
using System;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;

public class HashChecker
{
    public bool ContainsSubstringInHash(string input, string substring)
    {
        if (input == null || input.Length == 0)
        {
            return false;
        }

        using (var md5 = MD5.Create())
        {
            var hash = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(md5.ComputeHash(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(input)));

            if (substring == null || substring.Length == 0)
            {
                return false;
            }

            var substringBytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(substring);
            var substringHash = md5.ComputeHash(substringBytes);

            // Use constant-time comparison to avoid timing attacks
            var equal = Security.Cryptography.HashAlgorithm.ConstantTimeEquals(hash, substringHash);

            return equal;
        }
    }
}
```
This implementation uses the `constantTimeEquals` method from the `Security.Cryptography.HashAlgorithm` class to compare the hashes in a constant-time manner, which helps to prevent timing attacks. This makes the function more secure and resistant to potential vulnerabilities."]

